    "https://dal.peopleadmin.ca/postings/21619": {
      "title": "JOBS AT DALHOUSIE UNIVERSITY",
      "description": "Dalhousie University | Donald Hill Family Postdoctoral Fellowship in the Faculty of Medicine Skip to Main Content JOBS AT DALHOUSIE UNIVERSITY Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help About Dalhousie The Dalhousie Impact Working at Dal Equity, Diversity and Inclusion Talent Pathways Student Employment Hiring a Student FAQS for applicants Donald Hill Family Postdoctoral Fellowship in the Faculty of Medicine Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Position Details Position Information Position Title Donald Hill Family Postdoctoral Fellowship in the Faculty of Medicine Posting Number PTAP3379P Department/Unit Medical Research Development Office Location Halifax, Nova Scotia, Canada Job Summary & Key Responsibilities Dr. Donald Hill, a Dalhousie alumnus with a distinguished career in business, medicine and research, created three prestigious postdoctoral fellowships in 2018 in the areas of Medicine, Computer Science (in collaboration with Engineering) and Arts and Social Sciences. These fellowships have been created to accelerate the careers of recent doctoral graduates engaged in leading-edge research who have also demonstrated an interest in the impact of technology on broader society. While the primary focus of the postdoctoral fellowships is advancement within each individual field of Medicine, Computer Science and Humanities/Social Sciences, there is an expectation that fellows will become engaged and appreciate the necessity and benefits of interfacing with a wide diversity of disciplines, knowledge, and cultures to recognize and solve emerging challenges. The intent is not to develop answers, but to become skilled at framing the entire scope of these challenges and exploring/inventing different vehicles and methodologies to seek comprehensive solutions. Position Summary: Dalhousie University is currently seeking applications for the prestigious Donald Hill Family postdoctoral fellowship position within the Faculty of Medicine. The successful candidate will have a strong research track record in their field of study and will have research interests which align with and support on-going initiatives within the Faculty of Medicine. Applicants will also have a demonstrable interest in broader societal impact of emerging technology and promotion of a culture of accountability for the future of society. The successful applicant will join Dalhousie as a Donald Hill Family Postdoctoral Fellow, working with other Fellows from the Faculties of Computer Science and Arts & Social Sciences to meet the objectives of this fellowship program while engaging full-time in their own research. Qualifications/Requirements of Position Eligibility Requirements PhD completed within the last three years, or will complete your doctoral studies before the start of the fellowship Canadian citizen or permanent resident Written support of a supervisor whose primary appointment is in the Faculty of Medicine at Dalhousie University Evaluation Criteria Applicants are evaluated and selected based on the following criteria weighted as indicated: Research excellence and leadership in the research domain (40%) Quality of the applicant’s proposed research program (40%) Supervisor’s commitment, synergy with institutional strategic priorities, and alignment with Fellowship objectives (20%) To be considered, applicants must include all of the following documents: CV in either CIHR Biosketch CV or Tri-Agency Narrative CV format Cover letter Letter of support from the applicant’s proposed supervisor (append this document to your Cover Letter) Proof of completion date for PhD (transcripts or other official documentation) Research proposal up to 3 pages in length PLUS a one-page summary outlining the applicant’s interest in the societal impact of technology and any activities they may have already engaged in relevant to this area. Contact information for two (2) references Salary Range/Pay Rate $70,000 (inclusive of benefits) Additional Information Dalhousie University commits to achieving inclusive excellence through continually championing equity, diversity, inclusion, and accessibility. The university encourages applications from Indigenous Peoples of Turtle Island (especially Mi’kmaq), persons of Black/African descent (especially African Nova Scotians), and members of other racialized groups, persons with disabilities, women, persons identifying as members of 2SLGBTQIA+ communities, and all candidates who would contribute to the diversity of our community. In accordance with our Employment Equity Policy, preference will be given in hiring processes to candidates who self-identify as members of one or more of the equity-deserving groups listed above. For more information, including details related to our Employment Equity Policy and Plan and definitions of equity-deserving groups please review our Employment Equity information. If you require any support for the purpose of accommodation, such as technical aids or alternative arrangements, please let us know of these needs and how we can be of assistance. Dalhousie University is committed to ensuring all candidates have full, fair, and equitable participation in the hiring process. Our complete Accommodation Policy can be viewed here . Posting Detail Information Term of appointment Three years with an anticipated start date of January 2, 2027 or earlier. Open Date 06/12/2026 Close Date 07/24/2026 Open Until Filled No Quick Link for Direct Access to Posting https://dal.peopleadmin.ca/postings/21101 Documents Needed to Apply Required Documents Cover Letter Résumé / Curriculum Vitae (CV) Research Statement List of referees Transcripts Optional Documents Dalhousie University Halifax, Nova Scotia, Canada  B3H 4R2 1-902-494-2211 Agricultural Campus Truro, Nova Scotia, Canada  B2N 5E3 1-902-893-6600 Contact Us | Campus Directory | Campus Maps Media Centre | Privacy Statement Terms of Use To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://dal.peopleadmin.ca/postings/22179": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Fellow",
      "description": "Dalhousie University | Postdoctoral Fellow - Black 2SLGBTQI+Health Skip to Main Content JOBS AT DALHOUSIE UNIVERSITY Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help About Dalhousie The Dalhousie Impact Working at Dal Equity, Diversity and Inclusion Talent Pathways Student Employment Hiring a Student FAQS for applicants Postdoctoral Fellow - Black 2SLGBTQI+Health Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Position Details Position Information Position Title Postdoctoral Fellow - Black 2SLGBTQI+Health Posting Number PTAP3540P Department/Unit Nursing Location Halifax, Nova Scotia, Canada Job Summary & Key Responsibilities Position The Postdoctoral Fellow will join Dr. OmiSoore H. Dryden’s research program in Black Health Studies, Black Studies, and Black queer and trans studies at Dalhousie University. The fellowship is designed as a mentored early-career research position that will provide opportunities to deepen expertise in Black queer and trans health research, mixed methods, community-engaged research, scholarly writing, and research leadership. The Fellow will play a central role in a multi-site Canadian research project examining Black queer and trans health, healthcare experiences, structural anti-Black racism, anti-Black homophobia and transphobia, and institutional accountability within healthcare systems. Grounded in Black Health Studies, Black feminist scholarship, and Black queer and trans theory and methodologies, the research uses community-engaged and mixed-methods approaches. Working closely with Dr. Dryden and the broader research team, the Postdoctoral Fellow will contribute to the intellectual, methodological, and day-to-day implementation of the research while developing their own scholarly expertise and publication record. Key responsibilities Contribute to the theoretical, methodological, and analytic development of Black queer and trans-centred health research grounded in Black queer and trans studies, Black feminist scholarship, and Black Health Studies. Take a central role in the day-to-day implementation and coordination of research activities across multiple Canadian sites, under the mentorship and direction of Dr. Dryden. Support research workplans, timelines, milestones, and coordination of interconnected study activities. Contribute to the development and refinement of research questions, instruments, study protocols, and research ethics materials. Coordinate and participate in qualitative and quantitative data collection, data management, analysis, and mixed-methods integration. Work collaboratively with research assistants, trainees, community-based research personnel, collaborators, and community partners. Support community advisory and governance processes, including community-engaged interpretation, validation, and dissemination of findings. Support and mentor research assistants and student trainees in research activities appropriate to the Fellow’s experience. Contribute to peer-reviewed publications, scholarly presentations, reports, policy and practice resources, and community-facing knowledge mobilization, with opportunities to lead manuscripts and other scholarly outputs. Maintain research documentation and progress tracking and work with Dr. Dryden to identify and address emerging research and implementation issues. The fellowship will provide structured opportunities for mentorship, publication, methodological development, interdisciplinary collaboration, conference participation, and the development of an emerging program of scholarship in Black Health Studies and Black queer and trans studies. The position is based in Halifax, Nova Scotia, and the successful candidate will be expected to reside and work in Halifax for the duration of the appointment. Qualifications/Requirements of Position This is a designated position for applicants who self-identify as Black/persons of African descent. Black applicants who also identify as members of 2SLGBTQIA+ communities are especially encouraged to apply. Required Qualifications A completed PhD, or PhD completed by the appointment start date, in epidemiology, implementation science, public/population health, health services research, or a closely related interdisciplinary health research field, with training or scholarship in Black Studies, Black queer and trans studies, gender and sexuality studies, or related areas. Mixed-methods research experience, with training and experience in qualitative and quantitative research methods. Evidence of research and scholarship in Black Studies and Black queer and trans studies, demonstrated through doctoral research, publications, scholarly presentations, research projects, or related academic work. Scholarly knowledge of Black feminist thought, Black queer and trans studies, anti-Black racism, anti-Black homophobia and transphobia, intersectionality, sexuality, and gender. Experience contributing to a funded research project, with familiarity with research ethics, study protocols, data collection, data management, and collaborative research processes. Experience with community-engaged research involving Black communities. Evidence of strong scholarly writing and the potential to contribute to peer-reviewed publications, presentations, and knowledge mobilization. Strong organizational, communication, and interpersonal skills, including the ability to manage competing tasks, meet deadlines, work collaboratively, and follow research activities through to completion. Assets Experience with implementation science, health services research, survey research, epidemiologic methods, or measurement development. Experience with REDCap, NVivo, and/or quantitative or statistical analysis software. Experience working on multi-site or multi-jurisdictional research. Experience supporting or mentoring students or research assistants. French-language proficiency. Salary Range/Pay Rate $62,000 Additional Information Dalhousie University commits to achieving inclusive excellence through continually championing equity, diversity, inclusion, and accessibility. The university encourages applications from Indigenous Peoples of Turtle Island (especially Mi’kmaq), persons of Black/African descent (especially African Nova Scotians), and members of other racialized groups, persons with disabilities, women, persons identifying as members of 2SLGBTQIA+ communities, and all candidates who would contribute to the diversity of our community. In accordance with our Employment Equity Policy, preference will be given in hiring processes to candidates who self-identify as members of one or more of the equity-deserving groups listed above. For more information, including details related to our Employment Equity Policy and Plan and definitions of equity-deserving groups please review our Employment Equity information. If you require any support for the purpose of accommodation, such as technical aids or alternative arrangements, please let us know of these needs and how we can be of assistance. Dalhousie University is committed to ensuring all candidates have full, fair, and equitable participation in the hiring process. Our complete Accommodation Policy can be viewed here . Posting Detail Information Term of appointment 2 years, anticipated start date of January 11, 2027 Open Date 09/11/2026 Close Date 10/11/2026 Open Until Filled No Quick Link for Direct Access to Posting https://dal.peopleadmin.ca/postings/22151 Documents Needed to Apply Required Documents Cover Letter Résumé / Curriculum Vitae (CV) List of referees Optional Documents Dalhousie University Halifax, Nova Scotia, Canada  B3H 4R2 1-902-494-2211 Agricultural Campus Truro, Nova Scotia, Canada  B2N 5E3 1-902-893-6600 Contact Us | Campus Directory | Campus Maps Media Centre | Privacy Statement Terms of Use To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
